 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Faster convergence compared to training from scratch due to pretrained priors [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Significantly reduced memory consumption after pruning redundant initial Gaussians [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Accelerated training, reduced memory footprint, high fidelity [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial feed-forward prediction [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When high-fidelity synthesis is required with limited training time or memory, and a good initialization method is available [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When no pretrained model or initialization method is available, or when the scene is too complex for the feed-forward predictor to handle [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** The fine-tuning phase typically converges faster than training from scratch due to the quality of the initial feed-forward prediction [S1]. However, the exact time complexity depends on the number of iterations required for convergence, which is reduced by the pretrained priors [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is initially high due to the redundant feed-forward Gaussians but is significantly reduced during the fine-tuning phase through pruning [S1].
+- **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat, which uses this fine-tuning approach, achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods on urban view synthesis tasks [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Accelerated convergence, reduced memory consumption via pruning, and high fidelity [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial feed-forward prediction; poor initialization may lead to suboptimal convergence [S1].
+- **When to use:** When training from scratch is too slow or memory-intensive, and a pretrained feed-forward model is available [S1].
+- **When not to use:** When no suitable pretrained model exists or when the scene is significantly different from the training distribution of the feed-forward model [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in the context of efficient volume-based Gaussian Splatting for urban view synthesis [S1]. It leverages pretrained priors to accelerate network training and enable faster convergence compared to other test-time optimization methods [S1]. The method is implemented using Adam optimizer and libraries like `torchsparse` and `gsplat` [S1].
+The concept of fine-tuning 3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced in the context of efficient volume-based Gaussian Splatting for urban view synthesis [S1]. The method leverages the observation that initial feed-forward predictions can provide a good starting point for optimization, allowing for faster convergence and reduced memory usage through subsequent densification and pruning [S1]. The ACO-EGR framework further extended this by integrating adaptive-scale color offset and error-guided reallocation for multi-scale rendering [S2].
 
